Chris Maloof wrote:

> The simplest way I can think of to write a bot that might occasionally
> do all right is as follows:
>
> Play a wizard.  Restart until you have 18 int, a ring of slow
> digestion, and a spellbook of identify.  Maybe a magic marker and
> charging scroll too for good measure.  Reasons: slow digestion lets
> you solve many problems with patience.  Dealing with unidentified
> items is very complicated in general, so the spell saves a lot of
> trouble.

In my opinion, this is not a bot. A bot should be able to solve NetHack,
not a very small subset of all possible NetHack games.

You could also #quit if you don't find at least two wands of wishing on
level 1... That's not a bot, that's cheating.

> Fight physically, don't cast spells.  It's too complicated to find
> enough books and non-metal armor, especially since you have to be able
> to fight physically anyhow when you run out of power.

Then why play a wizard? For the identify spell only?

> Don't have a pet.  It's too hard to keep track of it, especially while
> blind or hallucinating.

You should get telepathy, and when you have it, pets are no problem, even
for a bot. Hallucination is so rare that it shouldn't be problematic
either.

> Movement.  Explore dark areas at full health.  Search before moving.

Even with a ring of slow digestion, that would take too long, probably.

> Eating.  Eat anything safe [...] Later, eat things
> that might give you an intrinsic.

What else is there besides "anything"? Or do you propose no longer eating
only safe things after a while?

> Drink healing potions or return upstairs to heal
> if you calculate that you might not win.

There's *always* a possibility that you might not win, so you'd always
have to retreat. If you only retreat when that value exceeds a certain,
set value, in the long run that (small) chance would always come up.

> Sacrifice at altars. [...] This is possibly doable by
> hanging around an altar waiting for monsters to spawn.

And by carrying the wands of create monster you find, in addition to the
ones you mentioned earlier.

> Shopping.  Identify everything in a shop, then buy what you want.
> Don't bother trying to steal, because this involves either a pet or an
> angry shopkeeper.

Another reason to have a pet. Only really useful when you have a magic
whistle, of course, but in the long run, you will probably get one
somehow.

> Identifying.  Try the spell whenever you get enough power and have an
> unidentified item.  Once you get basic divination skill, don't leave a
> level until you've identified every item in it except for inferior
> weapons and armor.

You'll only know what weapons are inferior when you identify it. For a
beginning wizard, a +5 staff would be much better than a +1 silver dagger.

> Making holy water.  This is too important to skip, but unavoidably a
> bit tricky.  The easiest way might go something like, wait for a wand
> of digging; hit a fountain with it; drop vulnerable equipment; go
> diving until your potions are all water; dip or pray them into holy
> water.

Easier in my opinion would be to wait for a wand of cancellation. No risk
putting it in a bag of holding either: a bot doesn't make mistakes, only
misjudgements.

> Enchanting.  Be able to blank and write blessed scrolls of enchant
> armor, and guess when it's useful to do so.

A bot guessing? I'd call it judging.

> Special levels.  Sokoban, castle, endgame (ha!).

Special levels would be the least problem, since they're predictable.

> [...] I'm
> sure that the only way to solve the more general case would be to
> gradually build more features onto a simpler program.  It's an
> interesting thought experiment, anyway.

Well, anything a human can do *can* be programmed. The effort needed to do
so may be too great to fathom, though.

